ub.com/bjknauss/ona.py","stars":0,"forks":0,"watchers":1,"issues":0,"language":"Python","description":"Discord bot written in Python using the discord.py repo.","topics":[],"score":197403,"global_rank":314337,"on_pypi":false,"top_pypi":false,"python_rank":48412},{"name":"kmussar/DNA_Analysis_CodeAcademy","owner":"kmussar","html_url":"https://github.com/kmussar/DNA_Analysis_CodeAcademy","stars":0,"forks":0,"watchers":1,"issues":0,"language":"Python","description":"This is a learning exercise covering many aspects of python from the CodeAcademy course.  The scenario: A spy deleted important files from a computer. There were a few witnesses at the scene of the crime, but no one is sure who exactly the spy was. Three possible suspects were apprehended based on surveillance video. Each suspect had a sample of DNA taken from them. The computer's keyboard was also swabbed for DNA evidence and, luckily, one small DNA sample was successfully retrieved from the computer's keyboard.  Given the three suspects' DNA and the sample DNA retreived from the keyboard, it's up to you to figure out who the spy is!  The project should have methods for each of the following:  Given a file, read in the DNA for each suspect and save it as a string Take a DNA string and split it into a list of codons Iterate through a suspect's codon list to see how many of their codons match the sample codons. Pick the right suspect to continue the investigation on. My thoughts - 3 codons is not nearly enough to match DNA samples... but, regardless, here is the program:","topics":[],"score":197403,"global_rank":314347,"on_pypi":false,"top_pypi":false,"python_rank":48413},{"name":"kmussar/Bank_Account_CodeAcademy","owner":"kmussar","html_url":"https://github.com/kmussar/Bank_Account_CodeAcademy","stars":0,"forks":0,"watchers":1,"issues":0,"language":"Python","description":"This is a simple excercise demonstrating classes and objects. It is modeled after a bank account. The methods included are: accepting deposits and withdrawls, showing the balance, and showing the name of the account. Included are checks to make sure that funds are available in the account before they can be removed.","topics":[],"score":197403,"global_rank":314348,"on_pypi":false,"top_pypi":false,"python_rank":48414},{"name":"kmussar/Calendar_CodeAcademy","owner":"kmussar","html_url":"https://github.com/kmussar/Calendar_CodeAcademy","stars":0,"forks":0,"watchers":1,"issues":0,"language":"Python","description":"This is a a basic calendar program that the user will be able to interact with from the command line. The user can choose to (1) view the calendar, (2) add an event to the calendar, (3), update an existing event, or (4) delete an existing event. ","topics":[],"score":197403,"global_rank":314349,"on_pypi":false,"top_pypi":false,"python_rank":48415},{"name":"kmussar/Battleship_CodeAcademy","owner":"kmussar","html_url":"https://github.com/kmussar/Battleship_CodeAcademy","stars":0,"forks":0,"watchers":1,"issues":0,"language":"Python","description":"This project is a simplified, one-player version of the classic board game Battleship! In this version of the game, there is a single ship hidden in a random location on a 5x5 grid. The player will have 4 guesses to try to sink the ship.  To build this game I used knowledge of lists, conditionals and functions in Python. ","topics":[],"score":197403,"global_rank":314350,"on_pypi":false,"top_pypi":false,"python_rank":48416},{"name":"kmussar/Rock_Paper_Scissors_CodeAcademy","owner":"kmussar","html_url":"https://github.com/kmussar/Rock_Paper_Scissors_CodeAcademy","stars":0,"forks":0,"watchers":1,"issues":0,"language":"Python","description":"This program plays rock, paper, scissors.  The steps are:      1. Prompt the user to select either Rock, Paper, or Scissors.      2. Instruct the computer to randomly select either Rock, Paper, or Scissors.      3. Compare the user's choice and the computer's choice      4. Determine a winner (the user or the computer)  